[SECTION]
TITLE: 4. Transition Criteria

[SECTION]
TITLE: 4.1 Requirements to Design

[PLAN]
UID: SVP-TRANS-001
LEVEL: A
STATEMENT: >>>
**Transition Criteria: Requirements to Design**

- All high-level requirements reviewed and approved
- Requirements traceability to system requirements verified
- Requirements coverage analysis complete (no missing derived requirements)
- Requirements review findings resolved
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 4.2 Design to Code

[PLAN]
UID: SVP-TRANS-002
LEVEL: A
STATEMENT: >>>
**Transition Criteria: Design to Code**

- Software architecture and low-level requirements reviewed and approved
- Design traceability to high-level requirements verified (bi-directional)
- Low-level requirements satisfy high-level requirements
- Design review findings resolved
- Unit test procedures approved
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 4.3 Code to Integration

[PLAN]
UID: SVP-TRANS-003
LEVEL: A
STATEMENT: >>>
**Transition Criteria: Code to Integration**

- All source code reviewed and approved
- Code complies with coding standards
- Unit testing complete with 100% MC/DC coverage
- Static analysis findings resolved
- Code review findings resolved
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 4.4 Integration to Verification Complete

[PLAN]
UID: SVP-TRANS-004
LEVEL: A
STATEMENT: >>>
**Transition Criteria: Integration to Verification Complete**

- Integration testing complete
- Requirements-based testing complete
- Structural coverage analysis shows 100% MC/DC
- Robustness testing complete
- All verification procedures executed
- All problem reports resolved or deferred with rationale
- Verification traceability complete
<<<

[/SECTION]

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 5. Partitioning Verification

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Partitioning integrity will be verified through:

1. **Memory Protection Analysis:** Verify that Level A partitions have protected memory regions and cannot be corrupted by lower-level partitions. Static analysis tools will verify no pointer arithmetic crosses partition boundaries.

2. **Temporal Partitioning Analysis:** Verify that Level A tasks have guaranteed execution time windows and cannot be starved by lower-priority tasks. Worst-case execution time analysis will confirm timing budgets.

3. **Control Flow Analysis:** Verify that Level A code cannot be invoked by lower-level partitions except through defined, protected interfaces.

4. **Integration Testing:** Execute tests that attempt to violate partitioning (e.g., buffer overruns from lower partitions) and verify protection mechanisms.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 6. Compiler and Linker Assumptions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The qualified compiler (GCC with DO-178C qualification data) is assumed to correctly translate source code to object code according to the language standard (C99).

Verification of compiler assumptions includes:

1. **Tool Qualification:** The compiler is qualified as a verification tool (TQL-5) per DO-330, including test cases that verify correct code generation.

2. **Object Code Review:** Sample object code will be reviewed to verify:
   - Correct translation of critical algorithms
   - Absence of unintended compiler optimizations that affect safety
   - Correct implementation of language constructs used in the software

3. **Compiler Options:** Only qualified compiler options will be used. Non-qualified optimization levels are prohibited.

4. **Linking Assumptions:** The linker is verified to correctly place code and data in specified memory regions according to the link map.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 7. Reverification Methods

[SECTION]
TITLE: 7.1 Reverification Strategy

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Changes to previously verified software require reverification to ensure:
- The change is correctly implemented
- No unintended side effects are introduced
- Previous verification results remain valid

The reverification strategy includes:

1. Impact Analysis: Determine which software components are affected by the change
2. Selective Reverification: Reverify only affected components and their interfaces
3. Regression Testing: Execute regression test suite to verify no unintended effects
4. Traceability Update: Update traceability data to reflect changes
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 7.2 Impact Analysis Process

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Impact analysis is performed using:

1. Change Request Analysis: Review change request to identify affected components
2. Dependency Analysis: Use traceability matrices to identify dependent items
3. Coverage Analysis: Determine which test cases exercise affected code
4. Risk Assessment: Assess safety impact and determine verification scope
5. Reverification Plan: Document selective reverification activities required

The impact analysis results determine the scope of reverification:
- Minimal impact: Execute affected test cases only
- Moderate impact: Reverify affected component and interface tests
- Major impact: Full reverification of software
<<<

[/SECTION]

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: 8. Previously Developed Software

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The navigation algorithm component is previously developed software from a Level B certified system. To use this software in a Level A system:

1. **Assess Existing Verification:** Review previous verification evidence against Level A objectives
2. **Gap Analysis:** Identify Level A objectives not satisfied by existing verification
3. **Additional Verification:** Perform additional testing and analysis to close gaps:
   - MC/DC structural coverage analysis (Level B only required decision coverage)
   - Additional robustness testing for Level A requirements
   - Code review to Level A standards
4. **Configuration Management:** Verify configuration control was maintained
5. **Traceability:** Establish traceability from Level A requirements to existing software
<<<

[/SECTION]

